AB  - When allocating indivisible items across agents, it is desirable for the allocation to be envy-free, which means that each agent prefers their own bundle over every other bundle. Even though envy-free allocations are not guaranteed to exist for worst-case utilities, they frequently exist in practice. To explain this phenomenon, prior work has shown that, if utilities are drawn from certain probability distributions, then envy-free allocations exist with high probability (as long as the number of items is sufficiently large relative to the number of agents).
